      "text": "To find out when Wanda, Darren, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the lab again, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of their working schedules. Darren works every third school day, Wanda works every fourth school day, Beatrice works every sixth school day, and Chi works every seventh school day. Therefore, we have to find the LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n\nList the numbers as divisors:\n\\[ 3, 4, 6, 7 \\]\n\nCalculate the LCM:\n\n1. **Prime Factorization:**\n   - First, we find the prime factors of each number:\n     \\[\n     3 = 3\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     4 = 2^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     6 = 2 \\times 3\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     7 = 7\n     \\]\n   \n2. **Identify the highest power of each prime factor:**\n   - The highest power of 2 present in the factorization is \\(2^2\\).\n   - The highest power of 3 present in the factorization is \\(3\\).\n   - The highest power of 7 present in the factorization is \\(7\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the LCM:**\n   The LCM is found by multiplying these highest powers together:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 4 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 12 \\times 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 84\n   \\]\n\nThus, Wanda, Darren, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the lab again in \\(\\boxed{84}\\) school days.",
