      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine when Wanda, Darren, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the math lab, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their working schedules. Darren works every third school day, which means he works on school days that are multiples of 3. Wanda works every fourth school day, so she works on school days that are multiples of 4. Beatrice works every sixth school day, so she works on school days that are multiples of 6. Chi works every seventh school day, so he works on school days that are multiples of 7.\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\) is a multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 7. The LCM of a set of numbers is the smallest number that is a multiple of each of them. To find the LCM, we first determine the prime factorizations of the numbers:\n- The prime factorization of 3 is \\( 3 \\).\n- The prime factorization of 4 is \\( 2^2 \\).\n- The prime factorization of 6 is \\( 2 \\times 3 \\).\n- The prime factorization of 7 is \\( 7 \\).\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n- The highest power of 2 is \\( 2^2 \\).\n- The highest power of 3 is \\( 3 \\).\n- The highest power of 7 is \\( 7 \\).\n\nTherefore, the LCM is:\n\\[\n2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 4 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 84\n\\]\n\nSo, Wanda, Darren, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the lab in \\(\\boxed{84}\\) school days.",
